From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lennart Kurth <hi@le2ni.de>
Date: Sat, 05 Sep 2026 09:47:23 +0000
Subject: feat: carry per-job input tray and media selections to native printing

Electron exposes printer input tray and media IDs through webContents.print().
Chromium already has a media_type setting, but its desktop backends do not
consistently apply it, and there is no input tray setting. Carry the tray ID
through PrintSettings, dictionary conversion and printing service Mojo IPC.

On Windows, set DM_DEFAULTSOURCE and DM_MEDIATYPE in the job's DEVMODE before
DocumentProperties validates it. Verify explicit IDs against the driver's
advertised capabilities, because DocumentProperties can retain unknown media
IDs unchanged. Reject driver substitution of an explicitly requested ID. This
does not modify printer-wide defaults.

On macOS, encode the selected IPP media-source and media-type values
in a CUPS media-col collection, retaining an explicit paper size and quoting
values with CUPS option syntax and attach the collection to Core Printing.

On Linux, pass native PPD InputSlot/MediaType choices through GtkPrintJob,
including choices from CUPS-generated driverless PPDs. Preserve GTK's CUPS
submission, authentication and credential cache. GTK 3 cannot encode IPP
media-col collections. Keep explicit choices out of GTK's saved settings so
they do not leak into later jobs. Chromium still renders the PDF.


This should be proposed upstream as native printer media selection support.
Remove this patch once Chromium carries an input tray through settings and
IPC, applies both selections in its Windows, macOS and GTK print backends,
and has equivalent regression coverage for per-job settings and encoding.
Recheck the platform backends and printing_context Mojo traits on each roll.

